    },
    {
      "id": "Avgl",
      "code_hash": "d90e987e7e3265d82e6e3dc8581a20bf",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\"><strong>The question:</strong> how do missing and censored measurements change the set of molecules\nwe can certify against the same screening goals?</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><strong>Teaching example (invented values):</strong> imagine requiring solubility <strong>\u226510 \u00b5M</strong>.\nA reported value <strong>&gt;100 \u00b5M</strong> already establishes\na pass; <strong>&lt;5 \u00b5M</strong> establishes a failure. <strong>&lt;20 \u00b5M</strong> remains unresolved at this threshold.\nNone of these observations is an exact measurement at its assay limit.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">We use four endpoints from ExpansionRx's released campaigns: kinetic solubility,\nhuman liver microsomal clearance, Caco-2 permeability A\u2192B, and Caco-2 efflux ratio.\nThe targets below are <strong>editable teaching examples</strong>, not universal drug-selection\ncriteria. Passing them does not establish efficacy, safety, or clinical suitability.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
